      "thinking": "The real fault is that the cache is masking an expensive fallback: when it's unreachable, the page recomputes with quadratic complexity in both average_spend_per_item and typical_spend, which explains the jump from 26ms to 183ms. So while the port change triggered the incident, the underlying bug is that the cache became load-bearing because the recompute path was never optimized to handle real traffic.\n\nFor average_spend_per_item, the loop redundantly sums slices when it could just sum all purchase prices directly \u2014 a simple O(n) fix. For typical_spend_per_item, the repeated remove(min) approach is O(n\u00b2/2) and can be replaced by sorting once and indexing the median directly, while preserving the existing behavior like the ZeroDivisionError on empty input.\n\nNow I need to design a test that actually catches this regression rather than just checking correctness. I'm considering using a counting mechanism \u2014 maybe a custom price_cents property that tracks how many times it's accessed \u2014 so I can assert the number of accesses stays roughly linear rather than quadratic for a few hundred purchases. The average function's slicing would show up clearly this way, but the typical_spend function does comparisons on plain ints after extraction, so counting property accesses won't catch its inefficiency; I might need a different approach, like wrapping values in a counting int-like class or falling back to a bounded timing assertion despite the flakiness risk.\n\nLet me look at accounts.py to see how Account and Purchase are structured before deciding.\n\n",
